PART X — INNOVATION SEVEN: GOVERNED REMEDIATION AND VERIFIED EXECUTION

13. Build a coherent action control plane

Reuse the current automation and remediation infrastructure.

Do not create an unrestricted shell endpoint.

Define a registry of typed, explicitly supported operations.

Example action definition:

interface ActionDefinition {
  id: string;
  version: number;
  title: string;
  description: string;
  riskLevel: 'low' | 'medium' | 'high' | 'critical';
  requiredPermissions: string[];
  targetTypes: string[];
  inputSchema: unknown;
  isReadOnly: boolean;
  isIdempotent: boolean;
  requiresApproval: boolean;
  validateInput(input: unknown): ValidationResult;
  preflight(context: ActionContext): Promise<PreflightResult>;
  execute(context: ActionContext): Promise<ActionResult>;
  verify(context: ActionContext, result: ActionResult): Promise<VerificationResult>;
}

Use the existing TypeScript and JavaScript conventions rather than forcing every module into a new interface style.

13.1 Action lifecycle

Implement an explicit state machine:

draft → validated → preflight_passed → awaiting_approval → approved → executing → verifying → succeeded

Failure and blocked states must preserve the true error and evidence.

Reject invalid state transitions.

Do not allow a browser request to skip preflight or approval.

Revalidate the user's authority when executing the action, not only when displaying its button.

13.2 Preflight

Preflight must verify the inputs and prerequisites that can actually be checked.

Examples:

Target identity.

Authorization.

Supported operating system.

Required tool availability.

Target scope.

Required resource identifiers.

API availability.

Whether an action is read-only.

Whether a state change is reversible.

Whether another execution is already running.

A preflight cannot guarantee a remote system will remain in the same condition when the operation executes. Document that limitation.

13.3 Approval and risk

Require appropriate approval for high-impact or potentially disruptive actions.

The UI must display:

Target.

Proposed change.

Reason.

Expected effect.

Potential side effects.

Risk.

Required permissions.

Preflight findings.

Verification plan.

Rollback availability.

Do not promise rollback for an operation that cannot be safely reversed.

13.4 Idempotency and duplicate requests

Implement server-side idempotency or action deduplication for operations where duplicate execution could be harmful.

Persist:

